<body>
<h1>Unit 8: Elasticity</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Elasticity turns 'how stiff is this material' into a measurable number with units of pressure, and it separates the regimes in which a material returns to its shape from the regimes in which it does not.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Stress is force per unit area; strain is fractional change in length; Hooke's law says stress is proportional to strain only within the linear elastic regime.</li><li>Young's modulus E = stress/strain has the dimensions of pressure and belongs to the material, not to the particular specimen you cut from it.</li><li>The stress-strain curve has recognisable regimes: linear elastic, yield, plastic flow, strain hardening, necking and fracture.</li><li>Ductile materials give warning before they fail; brittle materials such as glass and cast iron do not, which is why toughness and ductility are engineered separately.</li><li>The elastic limit is not the tensile strength: the first marks the end of recoverable deformation, the second the maximum stress the material carries.</li><li>The elastic energy stored per unit volume is (1/2) x stress x strain, and Poisson's ratio couples longitudinal strain to lateral contraction.</li><li>Stiffness and strength are different properties: the modulus says how much strain a given stress produces, the ultimate stress says how much stress the material survives, and a material can be strong without being stiff.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Hooke's law needs small strains, an isotropic material, a uniform stress state and a fixed temperature; it fails for rubber (strongly non-Hookean), for plastics beyond the yield point, and for composites loaded off-axis.</li><li>Young's modulus depends on temperature and on rate: rubber's modulus rises with strain rate and falls with temperature, which is why a stretched rubber band stiffens when pulled faster.</li><li>Fatigue means cyclic loading below the yield strength can still fracture a component after many cycles, and a static tensile test cannot reveal it.</li><li>A hole or notch concentrates stress: the local peak stress, not the nominal stress, decides failure, so a small crack can dominate a design.</li><li>Poisson's ratio ties lateral strain to longitudinal strain, so no solid can be perfectly incompressible and perfectly isotropic at once; most engineering metals sit near 0.3.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 5 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Hooke's law: force constant</li><li>Stress; strain; elasticity and plasticity</li><li>Elastic modulus: Young modulus, bulk modulus, shear modulus</li><li>Poisson's ratio</li><li>Elastic potential energy</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Steel is stronger than rubber, so a steel wire is stiffer and will stretch less than a rubber band under the same load.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Stretch under a given load depends on the cross-sectional area and length as well as the material, and strength and stiffness are different properties. A thick short steel bar can easily stretch less than a thin rubber band.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Stiffness is measured by Young's modulus E (stress/strain) and strength by the ultimate tensile stress. Steel has a much larger E than rubber, so for identical geometry it stretches less, but strength tells how much stress it can bear before permanent deformation or fracture - a different question.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The elastic limit and the tensile strength of a wire are the same quantity.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> They are two different points on the stress-strain curve, and confusing them makes the curve unreadable. The elastic limit comes before the yield point; the tensile strength is the maximum stress, reached much later, in the plastic region.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The elastic limit is the largest stress for which the body returns to its original size and shape; beyond it permanent deformation begins. The tensile (ultimate) strength is the maximum stress the material can bear, and it lies well beyond the elastic region for ductile materials.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Poisson's ratio must be less than one for every material.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> It is bounded above by 0.5 from the requirement of positive compressibility, but the lower bound is not zero: auxetic materials have a negative Poisson's ratio and become wider when stretched.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For an isotropic elastic solid, -1 &lt; mu &lt; 0.5. Ordinary materials such as rubber (about 0.49) and steel (about 0.3) are positive; auxetic metamaterials have mu &lt; 0 and expand laterally when pulled.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A steel wire of length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 mm^2 stretches by 1.0 mm when a load of 100 N is applied. Find Young's modulus for the wire.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Stress = F / A = 100 / (1.0 x 10^-6) = 1.0 x 10^8 Pa. Strain = extension / original length = (1.0 x 10^-3) / 2.0 = 5.0 x 10^-4. Young's modulus E = stress / strain = (1.0 x 10^8) / (5.0 x 10^-4) = 2.0 x 10^11 Pa = 200 GPa, the accepted value for steel.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the expression for the elastic potential energy stored in a stretched wire, and show that the energy per unit volume is (1/2) x stress x strain.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> For a wire of length L, area A and extension x, the force needed is F = (E A / L) x. The work done in stretching from 0 to x is W = integral F dx = integral (E A / L) x dx = (E A / 2 L) x^2, and this is stored as elastic potential energy U = (1/2)(E A / L) x^2. Dividing by the original volume A L gives the energy density u = U / (A L) = (1/2) E (x/L)^2 = (1/2) x stress x strain, since stress = E x / L and strain = x / L. The area under the stress-strain curve is the energy density.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A wire is stretched to twice its original length. Without the stress-strain graph, explain what information is needed to decide whether the wire returns to its original length, and how the answer would differ for copper and glass.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> One needs the stress-strain curve of the material, and specifically where the applied point lies relative to the elastic limit. If the stress stays below the elastic limit the deformation is entirely reversible and the wire returns to its original length; beyond it, permanent (plastic) deformation remains. Copper is ductile: it has a long plastic region, so it can be drawn far beyond the elastic limit and still retain most of its shape on unloading. Glass is brittle: it fractures rather than plastically deforming, so exceeding the elastic limit usually means the wire snaps, with almost no warning.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Young's modulus of steel</td><td>about 200 GPa</td></tr><tr><td>Young's modulus of bone (along the shaft)</td><td>about 18 GPa</td></tr><tr><td>Ultimate tensile strength of mild steel</td><td>about 400 MPa</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk modulus of water</td><td>about 2.2 GPa</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the strength of a material be predicted from its atomic structure alone?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Dislocation theory explains strength qualitatively; quantitative prediction for real materials remains a research problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How do microscopic defects nucleate and grow into the cracks that end a component's life?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The mechanisms are known in outline, and fatigue-life prediction for specific parts remains an engineering challenge.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Plot a stress-strain curve for a steel wire or a plastic strip and label every regime you can identify.</li><li>Design an experiment to find Young's modulus of a bamboo strip, and state the dominant uncertainty in your result.</li><li>Explain, with an example, why a sharp crack is more dangerous than a round hole of the same size.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/elasticity">Read the chapter notes: Elasticity</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-elasticity-gas">Elasticity and gas unit suite 3D</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-3-stress-strain-and-elastic-modulus">OpenStax: Stress, Strain and Elastic Modulus</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-1-conditions-for-static-equilibrium">OpenStax: Conditions for Static Equilibrium</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-4-elasticity-and-plasticity">OpenStax: Elasticity and Plasticity</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
